---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { CONTACT, GA_ID, CONTROLLER, POLICY_DATE } from "../consts";
const analytics = Boolean(GA_ID);
---

<Base
  title="Privacy"
  description="What dynshift.com collects, why, for how long, who else receives it, and the choices and rights you have, including under India's DPDP Act."
>
  <section class="wrap prose">
    <h1>Privacy</h1>
    <p class="muted small">Last updated {POLICY_DATE}.</p>

    <p>
      This policy covers <strong>dynshift.com</strong>. PhonePad and LAYR have
      their own sites and their own policies, built on the same rules:
      <a href="https://phonepad.dynshift.com/privacy/">PhonePad</a>,
      <a href="https://layr.dynshift.com/privacy">LAYR</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>Who is responsible</h2>
    <p>
      <strong>{CONTROLLER}</strong>, an independent software studio based in
      India, decides what this site collects and why. In the terms of the
      GDPR it is the controller; in the terms of India's Digital Personal Data
      Protection Act 2023 it is the Data Fiduciary. Write to
      <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT}`}>{CONTACT}</a> about anything here; the
      <a href="/contact/">contact and grievance page</a> says how requests are
      handled.
    </p>

    <h2>In short</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>No accounts, no forms, no newsletter.</li>
      <li>
        {analytics
          ? "Visits are counted with Google Analytics only if you allow it. Until then nothing is loaded from Google Analytics."
          : "No analytics runs on this site."}
      </li>
      <li>
        The site carries advertising from Google AdSense. In the EEA, the UK and
        Switzerland Google asks for your consent first; in US states with
        privacy laws you can opt out of the sale or sharing of your data; a
        Global Privacy Control signal from your browser is honoured everywhere.
      </li>
      <li>DynShift never sells personal data and receives none from Google's advertising.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>What is collected, and why</h2>
    <div class="table-scroll">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>What</th><th>Why</th><th>Basis</th><th>Who handles it</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Your IP address and browser details, in server logs</td>
            <td>Delivering the pages and keeping the host secure</td>
            <td>Legitimate interest</td>
            <td>GitHub Pages, which hosts the site. DynShift does not receive these logs.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Your privacy choice, in your browser's local storage</td>
            <td>Not asking you again</td>
            <td>Strictly necessary</td>
            <td>Your device only</td>
          </tr>
          {analytics && (
            <tr>
              <td>Pages viewed, referrer, device type, approximate country, a random visitor identifier</td>
              <td>Counting visits and seeing which pages help</td>
              <td>Your consent</td>
              <td>Google Analytics, on DynShift's behalf</td>
            </tr>
          )}
          <tr>
            <td>Advertising identifiers in cookies, the page, approximate location</td>
            <td>Showing, measuring and protecting advertising; personalising it where allowed</td>
            <td>Your consent in the EEA, the UK and Switzerland; elsewhere Google's and DynShift's legitimate interest, with the opt-outs below</td>
            <td>Google, as an independent controller</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Your name, email address and message, if you write to us</td>
            <td>Answering you</td>
            <td>Legitimate interest, or taking steps you asked for</td>
            <td>DynShift</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    {analytics && (
      <>
        <h3>Analytics</h3>
        <p>
          Google Analytics 4 loads only after you allow it: in the EEA, the UK and
          Switzerland through Google's consent message, elsewhere through a
          two-button question from this site. Google Analytics does not store
          your IP address; it uses it to estimate your country and then discards
          it. Google signals and detailed location and device data are switched
          off, and event data is kept for two months. See
          <a href="https://policies.google.com/technologies/partner-sites" rel="noopener"
            >how Google uses information from sites or apps that use its services</a
          >.
        </p>
      </>
    )}

    <h3>Advertising</h3>
    <p>
      Advertising pays for the time that goes into the free tools DynShift
      publishes. It is supplied by <strong>Google AdSense</strong>. A few
      long-form pages carry one advertisement each, labelled
      <em>Advertisement</em>. There are none on the home page and none on this
      or any other legal page. Google may set cookies or read similar
      identifiers to serve, measure and protect those advertisements, and,
      where you have allowed it, to personalise them. Google handles that data
      under its own policy:
      <a href="https://policies.google.com/technologies/partner-sites" rel="noopener"
        >how Google uses information from sites or apps that use its services</a
      >. The cookies are listed on the <a href="/cookies/">cookies page</a>.
    </p>

    <h2 id="your-choices">Your choices</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Privacy settings</strong>, in the footer of every page, shows
        what applies to you and lets you change it at any time. Withdrawing
        consent is as easy as giving it.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>EEA, UK, Switzerland</strong>: Google's consent message asks on
        your first visit, with "Consent", "Do not consent" and "Manage options".
        Nothing that needs consent is used before you answer.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>US states with privacy laws</strong>: use
        <strong>Do Not Sell or Share My Personal Information</strong> in the
        footer (it appears where it applies). Advertising then stops being
        personalised for you.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Global Privacy Control</strong>: if your browser sends it, this
        site treats it as an opt-out everywhere: no analytics and no personalised
        advertising.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Anywhere</strong>: turn off personalised ads for your Google
        account at <a href="https://myadcenter.google.com/" rel="noopener">My Ad Center</a>,
        block or delete cookies in your browser, or use an ad blocker. The site
        works the same; nothing here detects or complains about it.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>How long it is kept</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Your privacy choice: up to 12 months in your browser, then you are asked again.</li>
      {analytics && <li>Analytics event data: 2 months at Google; only totals remain after that.</li>}
      <li>Advertising cookies: as listed on the <a href="/cookies/">cookies page</a>, set and expired by Google.</li>
      <li>Emails: while the conversation is active, then deleted within 12 months of the last message unless needed for a legal claim.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Who else receives data, and where</h2>
    <p>
      <strong>Google</strong> (advertising and{analytics ? " analytics" : " consent"}) and
      <strong>GitHub</strong> (hosting). Both process data in the United States and
      elsewhere, under their own safeguards for international transfers,
      including the EU Standard Contractual Clauses and the EU-US Data Privacy
      Framework. Nobody else receives personal data from this site.
    </p>

    <h2>Your rights</h2>
    <p>
      Wherever you are, you can ask DynShift what it holds about you, and ask
      for it to be corrected or deleted, by writing to
      <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT}`}>{CONTACT}</a>. You will have an answer
      within 30 days. In practice DynShift holds nothing about a visitor except
      emails they send;{analytics ? " analytics data is not linked to a name, but it can be deleted if you give your Google Analytics client identifier;" : ""}
      data held by Google's advertising is Google's to answer for, through
      My Ad Center and Google's own request forms.
    </p>

    <h3>India (DPDP Act 2023)</h3>
    <p>
      As a Data Principal you may ask for a summary of your personal data and
      how it is processed, and for its correction, completion, updating or
      erasure; withdraw consent at any time, as easily as you gave it, without
      affecting processing before the withdrawal; nominate someone to exercise
      your rights if you die or become unable to; and have a grievance
      answered. Raise a grievance first with DynShift through the
      <a href="/contact/">contact and grievance page</a>; if you are not
      satisfied, you may complain to the Data Protection Board of India. If you
      would like this notice in English or any language in the Eighth Schedule
      to the Constitution, write to us and we will provide it.
    </p>

    <h3>EEA, UK and Switzerland</h3>
    <p>
      You have the rights of access, rectification, erasure, restriction,
      portability and objection, and the right to withdraw consent at any time.
      You may complain to your data protection authority (in the UK, the ICO;
      in Switzerland, the FDPIC).
    </p>

    <h3>United States</h3>
    <p>
      DynShift does not sell personal data for money. Advertising cookies can
      count as "sharing" or "targeted advertising" under some state laws;
      opt out as described above. DynShift does not knowingly process
      sensitive data.
    </p>

    <h3>Brazil, Canada and elsewhere</h3>
    <p>
      The same rights of access, correction and deletion, and the same choices,
      apply to you. Write to <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT}`}>{CONTACT}</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>Children</h2>
    <p>
      This site is not directed at children and DynShift does not knowingly
      collect data from anyone under 18. If you believe a child has written to
      us, tell us and the message will be deleted.
    </p>

    <h2>Security and breaches</h2>
    <p>
      The site is static and served over HTTPS; there is no database to breach.
      If personal data DynShift holds (such as email) is ever exposed, the
      people affected will be told promptly, and regulators will be notified
      where the law requires it.
    </p>

    <h2>Changes</h2>
    <p>
      When this policy changes, the date at the top changes with it. The site's
      source, including every version of this page, is
      <a href="https://github.com/MNBLabs/dynshift.com" rel="noopener">public on GitHub</a>.
    </p>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  section {
    padding-block: clamp(24px, 5vw, 56px);
  }
  .prose {
    max-width: 78ch;
  }
</style>
